      "id": "1x7q1e1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: CONCEPT OF NATIONAL INCOME"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "National Income aggregates measure the monetary value of final goods and services produced within or by a nation.",
        "Gross National Product (GNP) accounts for Net Factor Income from Abroad (NFIA) in addition to Domestic Product (GDP).",
        "Net Domestic Product (NDP) and Net National Product (NNP) are obtained by deducting Depreciation from Gross figures.",
        "Market Price (MP) includes Net Indirect Taxes (NIT), whereas Factor Cost (FC) reflects the actual cost of production factors.",
        "Three distinct methods exist for measuring National Income: Value Added Method, Income Method, and Expenditure Method."
      ],
      "id": "qzcsuj0"
    },
